00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:03.980
The first one is Network
Interface Details.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:06.090
So Network Interface
Details gives you

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.770
a VMs view of the network.

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:12.360
You can, in a single page,
see the key attributes

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:16.120
for that VM instance and
the network interface.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:19.920
You can also view all the
firewall rules associated

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.770
with that specific VM
instance and the effect

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:27.600
that those firewall rules
have on that VM instance.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.270
So that should allow you
to very quickly diagnose

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.130
network connectivity issues.

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:36.240
And also verify if only the
intended ports and protocols

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.805
are accessible for that
specific VM instance.

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:44.250
The second product
is VPC Flow Logs.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:48.420
So VPC Flow Logs provide
responsive flow level network

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.880
telemetry for your
GCP environment.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.880
Think of it as analogous to
our net flow for those of you

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:56.570
who are used to that.

00:04:56.570 --> 00:05:00.210
VPC Flow Logs provides
near real time telemetry

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:04.230
without having any
performance impact to you.

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:08.040
So, it also provides
a granularity

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.870
that you might be
comfortable with.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:14.640
You should be able to collect
metrics for, say, a VPC

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.260
network or a subnet.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.810
Or drill all the way down to
be able to collect metrics

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:23.870
around a specified VM instance
or a virtual interface.

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:27.270
And it includes metrics
like throughput and RTT.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:34.480
The next product I want to talk
to you about is Firewall Logs.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:36.940
So firewall logs
get you visibility

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:39.190
into every single
connection attempt

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:42.010
against a specific firewall
rule that you have logging

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:43.180
enabled for.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:48.580
So you should be
able to see and audit

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:52.472
all the connections that were
allowed, and those that failed.

00:05:52.472 --> 00:05:54.430
And you should be able
to take that information

00:05:54.430 --> 00:06:00.700
and understand patterns,
perform some real time analysis,

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:02.650
as well as be able to
kind of ship it off

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:04.870
to Google Cloud Storage
for future forensics

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:05.830
and audit use cases.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:11.320
And the last one, the fourth
one I want to talk to you about

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:14.050
was Load Balancer Telemetry.

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:18.820
So this monitoring provides
you visibility into all

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:22.450
of your internet
facing applications.

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:25.210
You get metrics and visibility
for both the front end

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:26.380
and the back end.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:30.590
And it includes metrics
like number of requests,

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:33.850
latency, again, for the
front end and the back end,

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:36.700
and the connections that
are opened, that failed,

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:39.020
as well as throughput.

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:43.270
So, we talked about Network
Interface Details, VPC Flow

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:46.810
Logs, Firewall Logs, and
Load Balancer Telemetry.

