00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:20.607
So I wanted to begin-- go
through some practical use

00:07:20.607 --> 00:07:22.440
cases and things that
you encounter on a day

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:23.740
to day basis.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:25.200
One of the things
that we realized

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.090
when we were doing our
research and we were looking

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:28.465
at the problems
you encounter, is

00:07:28.465 --> 00:07:31.590
that you encounter a very
difficult or tricky problem

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:32.880
once in a while.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.520
The rest of it is
death by 1,000 cuts.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:40.080
So the typical use cases that
we find people struggling with

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.040
are very basic things,
like connectivity.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:43.800
So let's go through
use case number one.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:45.425
Imagine you're a
backend engineer who's

00:07:45.425 --> 00:07:47.400
decided to use a new
database for your project.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.900
You're developing it locally
and everything works just fine.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:51.520
Now you're ready stand
up a test environment,

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:53.145
you create an instance
to run your app,

00:07:53.145 --> 00:07:56.640
you create an instance to
run the database server,

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:58.510
you start the app, and it hangs.

00:07:58.510 --> 00:07:59.010
Right?

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:00.360
Of course, it's, like,
Friday afternoon, you've

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.100
been working all day
to do it, it's 4:30,

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:04.500
you're ready to go
home, meet somebody--

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:05.220
hang out.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:06.955
Unfortunately, it's not working.

00:08:06.955 --> 00:08:09.330
So let's go to the demo and
talk about how we can resolve

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:11.410
or troubleshoot what's going on.

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:14.133
OK, so, I am the
developer, I'm happy,

00:08:14.133 --> 00:08:15.550
I've set up my
entire environment.

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:17.580
Here's the IP address,
I'm ready to go.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:19.960
I hit Enter, and it's hanging.

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:22.650
OK, It might still
be-- oh, oh, no.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:23.670
An error has occurred.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:25.140
OK, what do we do now?

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:26.600
So if you are a
network admin, you

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:27.840
are sure it's an
application issue.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.215
And if you're a
developer, you're

00:08:29.215 --> 00:08:30.502
sure it's a network issue.

00:08:30.502 --> 00:08:31.110
Right?

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:32.943
That is one thing that's a fact.

00:08:32.943 --> 00:08:34.860
So the developer will
probably, at this point,

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:36.539
start looking at
application logs,

00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:38.309
trying to figure
out what's going on.

00:08:38.309 --> 00:08:40.650
And the root cause
of the time delays,

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:42.960
here, are mostly
because we don't

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:44.240
know where the problem is.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:44.740
Right?

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:46.320
So we don't know if it's
a network problem, yet.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:47.778
But if we can rule
out the network,

00:08:47.778 --> 00:08:49.920
that gives us a really
good hint of where

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:51.265
we can go looking next.

00:08:51.265 --> 00:08:53.640
So let's see how we can use
the Network Interface Details

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.130
page in order to rule
out any possible issues

00:08:56.130 --> 00:08:57.870
with the network or
to prove that there

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:00.160
are issues with the network.

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:02.110
So this is my GCP project.

00:09:02.110 --> 00:09:03.040
Here's a web server.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:04.080
Here's the MySQL server.

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:05.628
Ignore the suspicious
host for now,

00:09:05.628 --> 00:09:06.920
there's nothing going on there.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:08.850
We'll look at that one later.

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:10.320
OK, so the web server is here.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:11.612
This is what I'm connecting to.

00:09:11.612 --> 00:09:13.360
And the database server is here.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:15.512
So let's click on these
three dots on the side.

00:09:15.512 --> 00:09:17.095
And right here,
there's something that

00:09:17.095 --> 00:09:18.220
says, view network details.

00:09:21.308 --> 00:09:22.850
First, I'll give
you a quick overview

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:24.050
of what the page
contains, and then I'll

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:25.157
show you how you use it.

00:09:25.157 --> 00:09:27.740
The top part are the attributes
that Manasa was talking about.

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:29.270
These are things that
we found were sprinkled

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:30.562
through the console everywhere.

00:09:30.562 --> 00:09:32.750
They were on the Details
page, some of these things

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:37.130
were buried in subtabs on
the Instance Details page.

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:39.110
It was very hard to pull
all of this together.

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:40.490
So just by pulling
it all together,

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:41.870
we found that we're
adding a lot of value

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:43.287
by showing you the
things that are

00:09:43.287 --> 00:09:45.050
the most important
to this instances

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:48.000
behavior on the
network that it's on.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:50.360
And beyond that, we looked
at firewall rules and Routes.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:52.487
So if you have a VPC
network in production,

00:09:52.487 --> 00:09:54.820
you probably have hundreds
of firewall rules, maybe even

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:55.882
thousands.

00:09:55.882 --> 00:09:58.090
If you wanted to understand
which firewall rules were

00:09:58.090 --> 00:09:59.770
applied to this
instance, in the past,

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:01.480
you would go to the
firewalls list page

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:03.120
and go through
all 1,000 of them.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.230
I'm not kidding, all
1,000, one by one,

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:07.720
decide which ones apply,
which ones don't, create

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:09.015
a list of those.

00:10:09.015 --> 00:10:10.640
So, we thought we
could do that better.

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:11.770
So we did that for you.

00:10:11.770 --> 00:10:14.062
So, these are the firewall
rules that are applying only

00:10:14.062 --> 00:10:15.200
to the specific instance.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:17.920
Taking things like tags
and other things-- service

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:19.430
accounts-- into account.

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:19.930
Right?

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:22.013
We did the same
thing for Routes.

00:10:22.013 --> 00:10:23.680
So now we have a list
of firewall rules.

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:26.180
But that still means you have
to go through each one of them

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:28.930
and apply those firewall
rules in the order

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:32.207
that the control-- or the data
plan actually applies them.

00:10:32.207 --> 00:10:34.540
So we took it a step further,
and we created the network

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:35.590
analysis section.

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:37.007
This is what's
here at the bottom.

00:10:37.007 --> 00:10:38.900
So let me explain
what's going on here.

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:41.450
It's not necessarily
the easiest concept.

00:10:41.450 --> 00:10:43.720
So we are creating a
two-dimensional grid.

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:46.330
On one dimension, we have
all possible ingress sources.

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:48.580
On the other dimension, we
have all possible protocols

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:49.540
and ports.

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:52.270
We run through all of the
firewall rules, one by one,

00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:56.073
and then we open and close
port source combinations,

00:10:56.073 --> 00:10:57.490
and then we come
up with a model--

00:10:57.490 --> 00:11:00.020
a two-dimensional model-- of
what can access this instance.

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:02.380
We do it for Egress and we
do it for Routes, as well.

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:05.350
After we do that, we dump all
of that out into a tabular

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:09.080
form, most specific
IPs to least specific.

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:11.830
So I happen to know the IP
address of my database server--

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:13.420
it's 10.0.0.3.

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:13.947
Right?

00:11:13.947 --> 00:11:16.030
I can check to see whether
the database server can

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:17.020
access this instance.

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:18.562
Actually, I'm not
interested in that,

00:11:18.562 --> 00:11:21.260
I'm interested to see if this
can access the database server.

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:23.980
So I'll look at Egress
And see, hm, this instance

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:25.090
can access everything.

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:27.340
So it's certainly not an
outbound firewall rule.

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:30.410
It could be an inbound
firewall rule on the database.

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:32.650
So let's go to the same
page on the database server.

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:34.108
All right, that
was the web server,

00:11:34.108 --> 00:11:36.370
here's the database server.

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:41.930
View network details,
scroll down to the analysis,

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:46.130
and find web server has
an IP address of 10.0.0.5.

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:48.950
So, scrolling down, here it is.

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:51.497
Everything is blocked,
except for these ports.

00:11:51.497 --> 00:11:54.080
Now it's a MySQL database, so,
if you know what MySQL runs on,

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:56.840
it's 3306 It's not
there, it's missing.

00:11:56.840 --> 00:11:59.600
That's the problem,
it's a firewall rule.

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:02.360
I can go up here and find, oh,
man, there is a firewall rule.

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:05.420
Someone did create it,
it's just disabled.

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:07.810
So I can click on
firewall rule, edit it.

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:11.520
Turn it back on.

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:17.540
Patiently wait.

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:22.730
Let's refresh, just in case.

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:32.280
Firewall rule is active.

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:35.360
I go to the analysis, and I
see, hm, 3306 is now allowed.

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:36.490
That looks good.

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:40.212
Let's go back to the error
page, refresh, and it works.

00:12:40.212 --> 00:12:42.170
I know it's hard to read,
but it says it works.

00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:43.140
It's pretty happy in
the corner, there.

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:44.040
SPEAKER 1: You're good.

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:45.910
GREG FRANCYK: All right, so
that was a use case number one.

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:48.070
Let's go to use case number two,
can we go back to the slides,

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:48.570
please?

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:53.930
All right, use case number
two is about network security.

00:12:53.930 --> 00:12:55.570
So you're a network security
admin in your company.

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:57.195
Your company has a
process for auditing

00:12:57.195 --> 00:12:59.350
all new sensitive systems,
like database servers,

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:03.040
to ensure that access is limited
to what's absolutely necessary,

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:05.290
and you're leading an audit
for a new database server.

00:13:05.290 --> 00:13:07.123
So the one we just stood
up in use case one.

00:13:07.123 --> 00:13:09.700
You want to verify, obviously,
that only necessary ports are

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:12.047
open for the documentation
of the dev team,

00:13:12.047 --> 00:13:14.380
you want to understand what's
connecting to that server,

00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:16.255
and you want to understand
what your security

00:13:16.255 --> 00:13:17.430
posture is blocking.

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:20.870
All right, so if we could
go back to the demo.

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:24.290
I won't go through the network
interface details page again,

00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:26.540
but I think you can see how
this would be very helpful

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:29.627
in understanding what can and
cannot access an instance.

00:13:29.627 --> 00:13:32.210
Let's look at some things that
actually show what's happening.

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:34.543
All right, so we talked about
VPC Flow Logs and firewall

00:13:34.543 --> 00:13:35.580
logs earlier.

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:37.400
So this is actual
information of what's

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:38.600
happening on the network.

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:41.870
Every single packet that's
moving around your network.

00:13:41.870 --> 00:13:45.060
Well, not every single packet,
every single flow is captured.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:45.560
Right?

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:48.680
And this is an example
of a particular flow.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:51.380
So I've gone to the Stackdriver
logging section in logs.

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:53.240
I have VPC Flow Logs enabled.

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:55.820
I've hit subnetwork, that's
the resource that this is on.

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:59.260
And then, I look at vpc_flows.

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:01.780
Here is one object that
represents a flow that

00:14:01.780 --> 00:14:02.780
occurred on the network.

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:04.155
You see some
helpful information.

00:14:04.155 --> 00:14:06.620
So the 5-tuple is
nicely located, here.

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:09.730
And then some annotations,
like the destination instance

00:14:09.730 --> 00:14:11.030
and the destination VPC.

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:13.280
So annotations that you can use.

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:15.140
So this is actual
traffic on the network.

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:17.330
We do the same
thing for firewalls.

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:19.250
So every firewall rule
that has this enabled

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:20.840
will generate logs.

00:14:20.840 --> 00:14:23.000
So here is a firewall
rule that denied

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:25.620
a particular piece of traffic.

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:27.300
We have information
about the instance.

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:29.008
We have information
about the connection.

00:14:29.008 --> 00:14:32.273
Again, the 5-tuple,
so, nice and familiar.

00:14:32.273 --> 00:14:34.690
And then some information about
the firewall rule, itself.

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:36.510
Which firewall rule it
was, what the action

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:39.010
is, effectively, metadata
that's on the firewall rule.

00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:39.510
All right?

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:40.560
This is interesting.

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:42.690
However, there will be
thousands of these records,

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:44.607
and we're obviously not
going to go one by one

00:14:44.607 --> 00:14:45.822
and look at what's going on.

00:14:45.822 --> 00:14:47.280
So you can take
this a step further

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:50.670
and export this into
BigQuery, or really anything

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:51.660
that does analysis.

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:53.700
I've chosen BigQuery
for obvious reasons--

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:55.590
it was easy, it was
a one-click option--

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:56.850
operation.

00:14:56.850 --> 00:14:59.250
When I did that, I
constructed a query that

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:03.060
will take all
source IP addresses,

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:06.555
the action of the firewall
rule, the reference, as in name

00:15:06.555 --> 00:15:08.430
of the firewall rule,
and the amount of times

00:15:08.430 --> 00:15:09.780
that firewall rule was hit.

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:12.810
So effectively, creating
a firewall hit counter.

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:14.662
This coming from
the firewall logs.

00:15:14.662 --> 00:15:16.620
And I'm looking at things
that are specifically

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:18.300
trying to access the
MySQL server we've

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:19.680
been looking at earlier.

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:22.260
A quick group by, and
then I order by counts.

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:28.050
I hit Run, and we give
it a couple seconds,

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:29.340
and let's look at the results.

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:32.080
Actually, I could
just hide the editor.

00:15:32.080 --> 00:15:34.420
I instantly notice
that this IP address--

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:37.490
10.0.0.4-- is
being denied a lot.

00:15:37.490 --> 00:15:37.990
All right?

00:15:37.990 --> 00:15:40.980
So today, over 14 million times.

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:42.880
So this instance,
this source IP address

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:44.800
is trying to access
that database

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:47.570
server that we were looking at
earlier, and that's a problem.

00:15:47.570 --> 00:15:49.060
So I go back, and
I take a look at,

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:50.950
what could that possibly be?

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:53.830
And if you were paying attention
earlier, you've guessed it,

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:55.810
that is suspicious host.

00:15:55.810 --> 00:15:57.663
Now, why suspicious
host showing up here?

00:15:57.663 --> 00:15:59.830
Because I'm running end map
from the suspicious host

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:01.330
against MySQL server.

00:16:01.330 --> 00:16:02.980
So we got that
very, very quickly.

00:16:02.980 --> 00:16:04.540
We see what's being blocked.

00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:07.390
And we actually see that
some things are successfully

00:16:07.390 --> 00:16:08.260
passing through.

00:16:08.260 --> 00:16:10.010
I could augment this
query and show things

00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:11.560
like port information.

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:14.290
Effectively, anything that
was on the firewall logging

00:16:14.290 --> 00:16:16.390
record from before.

00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:16.923
All right.

00:16:16.923 --> 00:16:17.840
And one more use case.

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:21.530
So could we please go
back to the slides?

00:16:21.530 --> 00:16:22.690
Thank you.

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:24.040
Use case number three.

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:25.873
So we talked about
internal use cases a lot.

00:16:25.873 --> 00:16:27.957
But let's say your application
goes to production.

00:16:27.957 --> 00:16:29.710
So you've put it
behind an HTTPS load

00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:31.940
balancer for a lot of reasons.

00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:34.330
What tools can you use to
monitor and gain visibility

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:35.513
into that traffic?

00:16:35.513 --> 00:16:37.180
So in this example,
let's pretend you're

00:16:37.180 --> 00:16:38.463
a Global eCommerce Company.

00:16:38.463 --> 00:16:39.880
You're deployed
in three regions--

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:42.010
the US, Europe, and Asia.

00:16:42.010 --> 00:16:44.470
And the site is made up of
different smaller applications,

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:46.250
so it's pretty complicated.

00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:48.550
And if you've been to any
of the load balancing talks

00:16:48.550 --> 00:16:51.670
that we've had about HTTPS load
balancer, you know it's global.

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:52.930
So we give you one IP address.

00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:54.940
Customers connect to
that one IP address.

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:58.762
And it's routed to the resources
that are closest to them.

00:16:58.762 --> 00:17:00.220
So, obviously, if
you're in the US,

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:01.750
you want to be served
by US resources,

00:17:01.750 --> 00:17:03.380
and the same thing
with Asia and Europe.

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:04.910
So how can we make
sure that's happening?

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:06.243
Well, let's go back to the demo.

00:17:08.734 --> 00:17:10.609
All right, so here's a
list of load balancers

00:17:10.609 --> 00:17:11.940
that I have in this project.

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:13.315
The one that we're
talking about,

00:17:13.315 --> 00:17:15.960
shopping-site-lb,
I'll click on it.

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:17.460
Configuration is
pretty complicated.

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:20.040
There's browse backends, there's
carts, there's a checkout,

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:23.060
something called
feeds, test-https.

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:24.390
And I'll go to Monitoring.

00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:26.450
And in Monitoring,
we see right away,

00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:29.240
the number of requests per
second across the backends that

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:30.920
are in this load balancer.

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:33.800
If I look at a specific
backend, let's say browse,

00:17:33.800 --> 00:17:36.110
I get this cool Sankey
diagram at the bottom.

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:38.240
And the Sankey diagram
is showing something

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:41.330
very interesting-- the locations
here on the left hand side,

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:44.460
are the entry points of traffic
onto the Google Network.

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:44.960
Right?

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:46.940
So customers in North
America, their traffic

00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:48.440
is entering in North America.

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:50.750
And typically, there are
resources that are configured.

00:17:50.750 --> 00:17:52.970
For the purpose of this
demo, I've taken them down.

00:17:52.970 --> 00:17:54.512
And as a result of
that, that traffic

00:17:54.512 --> 00:17:55.940
is being automatically
distributed

00:17:55.940 --> 00:17:58.130
by the Google load
balancing infrastructure,

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:00.175
mostly to Europe, and
a little bit to Asia.

00:18:00.175 --> 00:18:01.550
The traffic
highlighted in yellow

00:18:01.550 --> 00:18:03.372
is effectively out of region.

00:18:03.372 --> 00:18:04.830
So it's being served
out of region,

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:06.730
and this is much
higher latency for you.

00:18:06.730 --> 00:18:08.962
The customer has to go
all the way to Europe

00:18:08.962 --> 00:18:11.420
and the request has come all
the way back in order for them

00:18:11.420 --> 00:18:12.885
to be served.

00:18:12.885 --> 00:18:15.510
On top of this, there's a bunch
of other load balancing metrics

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:16.385
that you can look at.

00:18:16.385 --> 00:18:17.808
I won't go through all of them.

00:18:17.808 --> 00:18:19.350
I realize it's
probably hard to read.

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:22.020
But we have things like
latency, latency to the backend,

00:18:22.020 --> 00:18:24.150
latency to the frontend,
in addition to things

00:18:24.150 --> 00:18:26.108
like requests and throughput.

00:18:26.108 --> 00:18:27.900
All right, so those
are the three use cases

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:28.800
I want to go through.

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:30.383
Could we please go
back to the slides?

00:18:30.383 --> 00:18:32.453
And I'd love to ask Arunima
to come and join me.

00:18:32.453 --> 00:18:34.620
Arunima is UX Researcher,
and she'll talk about some

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:36.690
of the research that
we've done on this topic.

