started to just be
immersed in Google,

00:22:21.100 --> 00:22:23.390
and decided, why not
be in GCP, as well?

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:24.640
ARUNIMA KASHYAP: That's great.

00:22:24.640 --> 00:22:26.715
So it looks like you are
using the whole ecosystem.

00:22:26.715 --> 00:22:27.798
JASON CRADIT: A lot of it.

00:22:27.798 --> 00:22:28.570
[LAUGHS]

00:22:28.570 --> 00:22:29.903
ARUNIMA KASHYAP: That's amazing.

00:22:29.903 --> 00:22:32.530
So, next, Jason, we
would like to talk

00:22:32.530 --> 00:22:36.520
through some of the use
cases that Greg demoed on,

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:37.860
and see what--

00:22:37.860 --> 00:22:40.000
like, understand your
perspective on them.

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:40.730
Sounds great?

00:22:40.730 --> 00:22:41.110
JASON CRADIT: Yeah.

00:22:41.110 --> 00:22:41.943
ARUNIMA KASHYAP: OK.

00:22:41.943 --> 00:22:45.250
So let's pretend
that you are actually

00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:50.890
working with a
developer on your team,

00:22:50.890 --> 00:22:53.290
and who's developing
a new database,

00:22:53.290 --> 00:22:56.620
and everything is
working great locally.

00:22:56.620 --> 00:23:00.350
But as you are getting ready
to test in the environment,

00:23:00.350 --> 00:23:02.800
and you create an
instance to run your app,

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:06.460
and then the instance on the
database and a new instance

00:23:06.460 --> 00:23:09.565
as well, what you see is
that your app is hanging.

00:23:09.565 --> 00:23:11.038
Has that ever happened to you?

00:23:11.038 --> 00:23:11.830
JASON CRADIT: Yeah.

00:23:11.830 --> 00:23:13.405
No, there's no
reason to pretend.

00:23:13.405 --> 00:23:15.340
This is a normal day in my life.

00:23:15.340 --> 00:23:17.320
And we have great developers--

00:23:17.320 --> 00:23:19.600
so, if there's
developers here, I'm

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:21.010
sure you're great, as well--

00:23:21.010 --> 00:23:24.070
but it always works on
their machine, right?

00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:25.030
Always.

00:23:25.030 --> 00:23:27.670
And yet, when they throw it over
to my team to go and implement

00:23:27.670 --> 00:23:31.100
and production or put it
into the cloud environment,

00:23:31.100 --> 00:23:34.480
we notice issues, obviously,
and it doesn't just work,

00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:35.210
as we'd hope.

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:36.793
And as Greg outlined,
it's typically--

00:23:36.793 --> 00:23:39.085
you know, they're ready to
get out the door on a Friday

00:23:39.085 --> 00:23:41.080
afternoon, and then my
team has to take it over

00:23:41.080 --> 00:23:43.615
to implement into production
or into a dev or staging

00:23:43.615 --> 00:23:44.115
environment.

00:23:44.115 --> 00:23:47.160
So this has happened
quite a bit, recently.

00:23:47.160 --> 00:23:49.420
We've been transitioning--
I mentioned,

00:23:49.420 --> 00:23:52.110
we started in the
Cloud in 2012--

00:23:52.110 --> 00:23:54.990
we started traditionally with
a three-tier architecture.

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:57.960
And now, more recently, we've
been moving to microservices

00:23:57.960 --> 00:23:59.850
as in architecture
and some serverless.

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:02.400
And so, there's a lot of
volatility in our environment

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:03.700
right now.

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:06.985
And so, through that
process, every little piece

00:24:06.985 --> 00:24:08.610
we have to take apart,
and deconstruct,

00:24:08.610 --> 00:24:10.920
and then start putting
into new environments.

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:13.980
And when we've used
Docker and containerized

00:24:13.980 --> 00:24:16.470
quite a bit of our application--
and what happens is,

00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:17.970
in a three-tier
architecture, you've

00:24:17.970 --> 00:24:21.840
got, kind of, your border
network security defense.

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:25.360
And then, each tier, your
application, and so forth,

00:24:25.360 --> 00:24:29.060
has its own firewall rules
and things like that.

00:24:29.060 --> 00:24:32.670
In a microservices architecture,
it's not quite like that.

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:33.270
Right?

00:24:33.270 --> 00:24:35.770
You start to have
millions-- probably not--

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:39.097
but thousands of firewall
rules that really define

00:24:39.097 --> 00:24:40.680
your environment--
define the security

00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:42.760
context of your environment.

00:24:42.760 --> 00:24:45.770
And so, when you run into
this problem and it's hanging,

00:24:45.770 --> 00:24:48.780
you just got to scroll through
pages, and pages, and pages

00:24:48.780 --> 00:24:51.430
of firewall rules and data
to try to figure it out.

00:24:51.430 --> 00:24:53.640
And, certainly, there's
tools out there,

00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:55.130
and we've got great
documentation,

00:24:55.130 --> 00:24:56.880
like everybody does,
about our environment

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:58.965
and how it should work.

00:24:58.965 --> 00:25:00.840
However, you still need
to solve the problem.

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:04.430
You still need to find the right
rule to change to modify it.

00:25:04.430 --> 00:25:06.480
And because I own
networking and security,

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:08.830
it's my team's
role to go do that.

00:25:08.830 --> 00:25:11.310
So, historically, we would
scroll through the rules,

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:13.110
we'd search for
IPs, and things like

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:16.072
that to try to find
protocols, and it's difficult.

00:25:16.072 --> 00:25:18.280
It was really, really
difficult. Very time consuming.

00:25:18.280 --> 00:25:21.570
So with the new tools, like Greg
mentioned, the network details

00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:23.350
now, it's like two clicks away.

00:25:23.350 --> 00:25:23.850
Right?

00:25:23.850 --> 00:25:26.392
So now we don't have to spend
all this time searching for it.

00:25:26.392 --> 00:25:32.100
It's really quick to see the
instances view of its network.

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:32.850
Just like that.

00:25:32.850 --> 00:25:33.480
It's amazing.

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:34.730
ARUNIMA KASHYAP: That's great.

00:25:34.730 --> 00:25:38.450
I'm very glad to hear
that the network details

00:25:38.450 --> 00:25:41.350
page is actually solving
some of your problems.

00:25:41.350 --> 00:25:43.923
And so, now you and your
dev can both have beer--

00:25:43.923 --> 00:25:45.090
JASON CRADIT: Yeah, exactly.

00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:46.798
ARUNIMA KASHYAP: --at
the end of the day?

00:25:46.798 --> 00:25:52.360
OK, so now that you and your dev
are having a very happy time,

00:25:52.360 --> 00:25:55.540
let's move on to
the next use case.

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:59.520
So the next use case here
is, let's pretend, now,

00:25:59.520 --> 00:26:02.370
that you are working with
your network security admin.

00:26:02.370 --> 00:26:04.860
And you are, as Greg
mentioned, doing

00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:07.330
an audit for a very
sensitive system,

00:26:07.330 --> 00:26:11.790
and you wanted to verify
that all the necessary port

00:26:11.790 --> 00:26:14.700
that the dev team has
documented are open,

00:26:14.700 --> 00:26:18.180
and then, understand what
connecting to the server,

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:22.590
and determine what security
posture is blocking.

00:26:22.590 --> 00:26:26.460
I'm sure with your
passion in security,

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:28.577
you have definitely faced
some of this as well?

00:26:28.577 --> 00:26:29.660
JASON CRADIT: No question.

00:26:29.660 --> 00:26:30.590
ARUNIMA KASHYAP:
Can you talk to us--

00:26:30.590 --> 00:26:30.780
JASON CRADIT: Yeah.

00:26:30.780 --> 00:26:31.290
ARUNIMA KASHYAP: --a
little bit about it?

00:26:31.290 --> 00:26:32.640
JASON CRADIT: No question.

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:34.890
We are very interested
in cyber security.

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:36.600
And in our industry,
especially, we're

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:40.500
helping transport critical
infrastructure assets

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:41.527
around the US.

00:26:41.527 --> 00:26:43.110
And so, we care a
lot about protecting

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:44.132
our customers' privacy.

00:26:44.132 --> 00:26:45.840
But it's also interesting
to think about,

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:48.480
our customers are
always comfortable

00:26:48.480 --> 00:26:50.710
with us managing their
data in the cloud.

00:26:50.710 --> 00:26:53.520
So we've got an
extra burden on us

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:56.480
to help show how
we're protecting it.

00:26:56.480 --> 00:26:58.350
And in for years
in the cloud, it

00:26:58.350 --> 00:27:01.830
was really easy to sort of say,
yeah, that we trust our cloud

00:27:01.830 --> 00:27:05.027
provider to protect it, we've
blocked Port 22 to the world,

00:27:05.027 --> 00:27:05.610
so we're good.

00:27:05.610 --> 00:27:06.720
Right?

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:09.330
And then we had auditors come
in, and auditors are also,

00:27:09.330 --> 00:27:12.330
sometimes-- if they're
here, I apologize-- but,

00:27:12.330 --> 00:27:14.370
not always as
technically savvy as they

00:27:14.370 --> 00:27:16.620
go through a network
infrastructure in the cloud,

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:18.810
versus when we used
to do it on prem.

00:27:18.810 --> 00:27:21.930
And so, we run into
these two issues,

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:25.045
where we've got to
not only show Port

00:27:25.045 --> 00:27:26.670
22 is blocked from
the world, but we've

00:27:26.670 --> 00:27:28.810
got to prove it,
in some capacity.

00:27:28.810 --> 00:27:32.340
And forever in the cloud,
you could define the firewall

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:33.655
rules, and they were set.

00:27:33.655 --> 00:27:34.530
And you're like, see?

00:27:34.530 --> 00:27:34.950
We're good.

00:27:34.950 --> 00:27:35.450
Right?

00:27:35.450 --> 00:27:36.298
22 is blocked.

00:27:36.298 --> 00:27:37.340
But you want to prove it.

00:27:37.340 --> 00:27:38.965
And you won't prove
it's been like that

00:27:38.965 --> 00:27:40.080
for some amount of time.

00:27:40.080 --> 00:27:41.550
Now, with firewall
logging, you can

00:27:41.550 --> 00:27:44.310
start to go and look,
and see, and prove--

00:27:44.310 --> 00:27:45.360
Nope, absolutely.

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:49.230
We've blocked 14 million
packets, you know, today,

00:27:49.230 --> 00:27:50.430
or whatever.

00:27:50.430 --> 00:27:52.230
And that's a
functionality that's

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:54.630
been lost in the cloud
for quite some time.

00:27:54.630 --> 00:27:57.810
And now, it helps me rest
a little easier because we

00:27:57.810 --> 00:28:00.600
have more of a defendable
posture when we talk

00:28:00.600 --> 00:28:03.210
to our customers and auditors.

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:06.150
And it helps us just
convey the message

00:28:06.150 --> 00:28:07.872
and be more
transparent, I think,

00:28:07.872 --> 00:28:09.330
with how we're
protecting the data.

00:28:09.330 --> 00:28:11.370
ARUNIMA KASHYAP:
No, that's great.

00:28:11.370 --> 00:28:13.140
Thanks, Jason, for
expanding on that.

00:28:13.140 --> 00:28:15.960
And you were also mentioning
during one of our studies

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:19.378
that when we were actually
coming and talking

00:28:19.378 --> 00:28:21.420
to you about some of these
products, like network

00:28:21.420 --> 00:28:24.840
details, VPC Flow Logs,
and others, you were like,

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:26.100
do I need to use them?

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:29.730
And then, like, slowly,
you started using them,

00:28:29.730 --> 00:28:31.740
and now, you can't
live without them.

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:32.900
JASON CRADIT: Yeah,
it's really interesting.

00:28:32.900 --> 00:28:33.900
It's funny you say that.

00:28:33.900 --> 00:28:36.330
It's interesting to me that--

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:37.630
I grew up on prem, right?

00:28:37.630 --> 00:28:40.282
And I used to
implement firewalls,

00:28:40.282 --> 00:28:42.240
and built servers, and
network, and everything.

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:44.490
And there's tools you
don't realize you need.

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:46.460
And there's tools
that you can sort of

00:28:46.460 --> 00:28:47.630
say-- you take for granted.

00:28:47.630 --> 00:28:49.640
And you start out in
the cloud, there's

00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:51.440
a lot of energy and
enthusiasm, and then

00:28:51.440 --> 00:28:52.857
you're, like, yeah,
it just works.

00:28:52.857 --> 00:28:53.502
Right?

00:28:53.502 --> 00:28:55.460
But then you start to
really take it seriously,

00:28:55.460 --> 00:28:56.960
and you're like,
man, that's easier.

00:28:56.960 --> 00:29:01.380
I mean, It's so much better to
have instance level details.

00:29:01.380 --> 00:29:03.380
If we do that on prem,
or could have back then,

00:29:03.380 --> 00:29:04.280
it would have been amazing.

00:29:04.280 --> 00:29:06.322
But that's sort of the
power of the cloud, right?

00:29:06.322 --> 00:29:08.750
It allows you to
build new methods

00:29:08.750 --> 00:29:10.768
for solving old problems.

00:29:10.768 --> 00:29:11.810
ARUNIMA KASHYAP: Awesome.

00:29:11.810 --> 00:29:14.270
OK, last, but not the least.

00:29:14.270 --> 00:29:17.790
I know that when we have
talked to you previously,

00:29:17.790 --> 00:29:21.500
you have been like a big
advocate of monitoring

00:29:21.500 --> 00:29:24.170
and having visibility
into some of the metrics

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:25.610
that Greg was showing.

00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:28.880
So the last use case we had
is like, now your application

00:29:28.880 --> 00:29:32.420
is in production, and you
have a HTTP load balancer.

00:29:32.420 --> 00:29:35.420
What tools are you using,
currently, to monitor and gain

00:29:35.420 --> 00:29:36.680
visibility into traffic?

00:29:36.680 --> 00:29:36.900
JASON CRADIT: Yeah.

00:29:36.900 --> 00:29:38.000
ARUNIMA KASHYAP: If
you're using ad tools,

00:29:38.000 --> 00:29:38.790
how are you using them?

00:29:38.790 --> 00:29:38.930
JASON CRADIT: Yeah.

00:29:38.930 --> 00:29:39.500
No, for sure.

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:40.390
I appreciate that.

00:29:40.390 --> 00:29:44.900
It's interesting, as we moved
to microservices architecture,

00:29:44.900 --> 00:29:46.850
we put a load balancer
in front of everything,

00:29:46.850 --> 00:29:48.715
even if we don't need to.

00:29:48.715 --> 00:29:50.840
And we'd just started doing
that, mainly because we

00:29:50.840 --> 00:29:53.870
thought, we don't know about
our infrastructure, right?

00:29:53.870 --> 00:29:55.560
And if we start putting
a load balancer,

00:29:55.560 --> 00:30:00.150
we gain some of the Stackdriver
logging and things like that.

00:30:00.150 --> 00:30:04.250
But then, also, we get context
for how our traffic is working.

00:30:04.250 --> 00:30:06.170
And what's always
been interesting to me

00:30:06.170 --> 00:30:10.970
is load balancing is very much
a IT, sort of operations network

00:30:10.970 --> 00:30:12.230
problem.

00:30:12.230 --> 00:30:15.650
But you gain situational
awareness of your environment,

00:30:15.650 --> 00:30:20.072
like context for how your
traffic is actually shaping.

00:30:20.072 --> 00:30:22.280
So it's been interesting
for us to put load balancers

00:30:22.280 --> 00:30:23.238
in front of everything.

00:30:23.238 --> 00:30:25.770
So we can sort of
visualize the traffic.

00:30:25.770 --> 00:30:27.890
But then also understand
it, and understand

00:30:27.890 --> 00:30:30.170
how our customers are using it.

00:30:30.170 --> 00:30:32.567
And, as Greg showed,
you know, maybe we

00:30:32.567 --> 00:30:34.400
forgot to set up logging--
it never happens,

00:30:34.400 --> 00:30:36.530
guys, trust me-- but maybe
forgot to set up logging

00:30:36.530 --> 00:30:39.260
and we didn't know our North
America servers were down,

00:30:39.260 --> 00:30:41.390
or west coast or east
coast servers were down.

00:30:41.390 --> 00:30:43.220
And now, by visualizing
it in Stackdriver,

00:30:43.220 --> 00:30:45.608
we can see, oh, man, we're
routing to the wrong place.

00:30:45.608 --> 00:30:47.400
We're creating a problem
for our customers.

00:30:47.400 --> 00:30:50.040
It's adding latency
where it's not necessary.

00:30:50.040 --> 00:30:53.060
So we like to dashboard
it inside of Stackdriver.

00:30:53.060 --> 00:30:54.120
We do use another tool--

00:30:54.120 --> 00:30:55.610
I think it's called Sumo Logic--

00:30:55.610 --> 00:30:58.400
to do a lot of
our visualization.

00:30:58.400 --> 00:30:59.870
More for dashboarding sake.

00:30:59.870 --> 00:31:03.350
But my team predominantly
uses Stackdriver

00:31:03.350 --> 00:31:05.930
when they want to really get in
to the nitty gritty of what's

00:31:05.930 --> 00:31:07.280
happening in infrastructure.

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:08.530
ARUNIMA KASHYAP: That's great.

00:31:08.530 --> 00:31:10.340
Thank you, Jason.

00:31:10.340 --> 00:31:11.990
Anything else that
you would like

00:31:11.990 --> 00:31:14.510
to add that, you know,
some of these use cases we

00:31:14.510 --> 00:31:18.390
talked about maybe some other
stuff that we've not mentioned,

00:31:18.390 --> 00:31:20.120
and you would like
to mention to them.

00:31:20.120 --> 00:31:22.940
JASON CRADIT: Yeah, you
know, if I could shameless

00:31:22.940 --> 00:31:23.946
plug some things.

00:31:23.946 --> 00:31:29.240
[LAUGHS] We do take cyber
security really seriously

00:31:29.240 --> 00:31:31.370
at Pivvot, and we're really
interested in helping

00:31:31.370 --> 00:31:35.130
protect our customers data and
the data around their assets.

00:31:35.130 --> 00:31:37.790
So we use a lot of
tools to protect.

00:31:37.790 --> 00:31:40.990
All of the core
functionality of the cloud,

00:31:40.990 --> 00:31:44.690
like firewall logging, and
BigQuery, and things like that

00:31:44.690 --> 00:31:47.532
help us gain better context
into our environment.

00:31:47.532 --> 00:31:49.490
But we also use tools of
the application layer,

00:31:49.490 --> 00:31:50.940
like Trend Micro's
deep security,

00:31:50.940 --> 00:31:52.500
I think I saw a
booth for them here.

00:31:52.500 --> 00:31:54.500
It's a great tool and you
guys should definitely

00:31:54.500 --> 00:31:55.435
check out their booth.

00:31:55.435 --> 00:31:56.810
But I think more
than anything, I

00:31:56.810 --> 00:32:00.020
think I made a comment earlier
that it's interesting to me

00:32:00.020 --> 00:32:04.422
that you guys create tools
that we didn't know we needed.

00:32:04.422 --> 00:32:05.630
And I really appreciate that.

00:32:05.630 --> 00:32:07.380
And I know I've seen some
Googlers in the audience,

00:32:07.380 --> 00:32:08.990
and I guess I would
say, thank you

00:32:08.990 --> 00:32:11.510
for continuing to make
my job a little easier.

00:32:11.510 --> 00:32:15.555
It's hard in IT
Operations and Network

00:32:15.555 --> 00:32:17.060
to go get more resources.

00:32:17.060 --> 00:32:18.740
We want to hire more
developers, and we

00:32:18.740 --> 00:32:21.005
want to hire people who
can make our products--

00:32:21.005 --> 00:32:22.130
get them to market quicker.

00:32:22.130 --> 00:32:24.050
And that's good, we need that.

00:32:24.050 --> 00:32:26.750
But what I really
need is to make

00:32:26.750 --> 00:32:28.318
my job a little bit easier.

00:32:28.318 --> 00:32:30.110
So I appreciate the
tools you guys develop,

00:32:30.110 --> 00:32:32.843
and listening to us, and
